// apt-snapshot-guard: force a Timeshift snapshot before any package change.
//
// DPkg::Pre-Invoke runs once per dpkg-changing apt run, after the download
// phase and before the first unpack. It therefore covers install, reinstall,
// remove, purge, upgrade, full-upgrade / dist-upgrade and autoremove, and
// fires for every frontend that drives dpkg through libapt: apt, apt-get,
// aptdaemon (the GUI Software Updater / PackageKit), and unattended-upgrades.
//
// Pure metadata refreshes (`apt update`) use APT::Update::Pre-Invoke and are
// deliberately NOT hooked here -- no packages change. Simulations (-s) and
// --download-only never reach dpkg, so they never trigger a snapshot.

// The `test -x || ...` is load-bearing, not defensive habit.
//
// This file is a CONFFILE and the hook it names is not. dpkg deletes conffiles
// on purge, never on remove -- so `apt remove apt-snapshot-guard` deletes
// /usr/lib/apt-snapshot-guard/pre-invoke and leaves this line pointing at it.
// Every later apt run would then execute a missing command, get 127, and abort:
//
//   E: Problem executing scripts DPkg::Pre-Invoke
//
// including the reinstall that would have fixed it. The only ways out are
// editing this file by hand or `dpkg -P`, which is a poor thing to discover
// when your package manager has already stopped working.
//
// Guarding the call fixes it without a maintainer script -- and this package
// deliberately has none, an invariant check-deb.sh enforces so that nothing
// here can ever touch /etc/timeshift, where the SSH keys live.
DPkg::Pre-Invoke { "test ! -x /usr/lib/apt-snapshot-guard/pre-invoke || /usr/lib/apt-snapshot-guard/pre-invoke"; };
